WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:09.107
An order is saved, but the confirmation message never reaches the warehouse. The outbox pattern makes that impossible.

00:00:10.307 --> 00:00:11.236
Pause and think.

00:00:11.636 --> 00:00:15.293
Why not write to the database and then publish to the queue separately?

00:00:15.693 --> 00:00:23.008
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:30.841
This video explains how backends avoid lost or duplicated orders when a database and a message queue must agree.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:37.204
New word: queue. First-in-first-out structure. Picture it like this: A line at a bus stop.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.643
Picture a restaurant ticket rail.

00:00:42.043 --> 00:00:49.067
The waiter writes the order and the kitchen ticket in one motion, so one can't exist without the other. That is the outbox.

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:54.843
A runner carries tickets to the kitchen. If unsure whether a ticket arrived, they carry it again.

00:00:55.243 --> 00:01:00.131
The kitchen ignores any ticket number it already cooked. That is an idempotent consumer.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:10.285
New word: idempotent. Doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once. Picture it like this: Pressing an elevator button again doesn't call two elevators.

00:01:11.885 --> 00:01:19.106
And if the delivery can't happen, earlier steps are undone in reverse: refund the card, release the table. That is a saga.

00:01:19.185 --> 00:01:31.550
New word: saga. A long transaction split into steps, each with an undo step if a later one fails. Picture it like this: Booking a trip: if the hotel fails, cancel the flight.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:35.227
Let's walk a failure.

00:01:35.627 --> 00:01:39.760
The order and its outbox row are written in one database transaction.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.016
The relay publishes the event with a stable id.

00:01:43.416 --> 00:01:45.378
The acknowledgement gets lost.

00:01:45.778 --> 00:01:50.376
So the relay publishes the same event again. This is at-least-once delivery.

00:01:50.776 --> 00:01:55.745
The consumer has already seen that id, so it skips it. The effect happens once.

00:01:56.145 --> 00:01:58.409
Finally the relay marks the row as sent.

00:01:59.609 --> 00:02:00.131
Recap.

00:02:00.531 --> 00:02:03.701
Never write the database and the queue as two separate steps.

00:02:04.101 --> 00:02:07.259
Outbox plus relay gives at-least-once delivery.

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:09.876
Consumers dedupe by event id.

00:02:10.276 --> 00:02:13.167
Sagas undo completed steps in reverse order.

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:17.097
Practice in lesson forty point one and design sd twenty six.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:19.504
Back to the question.

00:02:19.904 --> 00:02:23.561
Why not write to the database and then publish to the queue separately?

00:02:23.961 --> 00:02:26.515
A crash between the two leaves them disagreeing.

00:02:27.715 --> 00:02:28.586
Quick recap.

00:02:28.986 --> 00:02:31.505
Write data and event in one transaction.

00:02:31.905 --> 00:02:34.123
A relay publishes the outbox.

00:02:34.523 --> 00:02:37.356
Sagas undo steps when later ones fail.
